ntdll: a commit granularity a container can choose.

Replaces the grow-on-fault quirk with the quantity that actually differs
between the machines this game works on and the one it does not.

The guest always sees 4 KB pages; the Windows ABI on ARM64 requires it. This is
a different number: how much Wine commits when the guest commits inside a region
it already reserved. Wine tracks protection in a 4 KB bitmap but must mprotect
at host page granularity, so on a phone with 16 KB host pages -- which Android
15 and later ship, and which this device's firmware says it can boot
(ro.product.cpu.pagesize.max = 16384) -- committing three 4 KB pages
unavoidably makes the whole 16 KB host page accessible. A program that writes a
little past its commit cannot fault there and never learns it was wrong.

Resident Evil Requiem is exactly that program. It commits 0x3000 and then copies
240 bytes where only 224 fit, running 16 bytes into the next page. 0045 measured
the allocation: the guest asked for 0x3000 and received 0x3000, so nothing below
it shorted anything. On a 16 KB-page host that overrun lands in mapped memory;
on this 4 KB kernel it is a real access violation.

So whether the game runs is a property of which kernel the phone booted with,
and a container can now say which of those two machines it wants to look like.

Defaults to 16 KB -- matching the phones this class of game is validated on,
rather than the kernel this one happens to boot. WINE_COMMIT_GRANULARITY_KB=4
restores exact Windows behaviour without a rebuild. Rounding up commits memory the guest did not ask for, up to three extra
pages per commit at 16 KB, and NtQueryVirtualMemory will report those pages as
committed where an allocator probing for free space can see them. That price is
paid by every container, which is the argument for the override.

The rounding never crosses the end of the view the commit belongs to. Growing
past it would turn a legal commit into STATUS_NOT_MAPPED_VIEW, which is the one
way this could break a program that is doing nothing wrong.

MEASURED, and stated here because it is the reason to expect little from this:
with the earlier fault-based version of this change the access violation
disappeared and fired exactly once in a session -- one call site, not a systemic
overrun -- and the game still did not proceed. It now reaches
VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST instead. This removes one obstacle; it is not known to be
the last one.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -5571,6 +5571,65 @@
  *
  * NtAllocateVirtualMemory[Ex] implementation.
  */
+/* Vessel: the granularity a commit is rounded up to, chosen per container.
+ *
+ * The guest always sees 4 KB pages -- the Windows ABI on ARM64 requires it, and
+ * Wine says so. This is a separate quantity: how much Wine actually commits when
+ * the guest asks for a commit inside a region it already reserved.
+ *
+ * WHY IT IS A SETTING AND NOT A CONSTANT. Wine tracks protections in a 4 KB
+ * bitmap but must mprotect at *host* page granularity. On a phone with 16 KB
+ * host pages -- which Android 15 and later ship, and which this device's own
+ * firmware reports it can boot (ro.product.cpu.pagesize.max = 16384) -- a guest
+ * committing three 4 KB pages unavoidably makes the whole 16 KB host page
+ * accessible. A program that writes a little past what it committed therefore
+ * cannot fault there, and never learns it was wrong.
+ *
+ * That is not hypothetical. Resident Evil Requiem commits 0x3000 and then copies
+ * 240 bytes to an offset where only 224 fit, running 16 bytes into the next
+ * page. Measured through patches/wine/0045, the guest asked for 0x3000 and got
+ * exactly 0x3000 -- nothing below it shorted the allocation. On a 16 KB-page
+ * host the overrun lands in memory that is already mapped and the game runs; on
+ * this 4 KB-page kernel it is a genuine access violation.
+ *
+ * So the difference between "this game works" and "this game does not" is a
+ * property of the kernel the phone happens to be booted with. A container can
+ * now say which of those two machines it wants to look like.
+ *
+ * DEFAULTS TO 16 KB, matching the phones this class of game is validated on
+ * rather than the kernel this one happens to boot. Setting
+ * WINE_COMMIT_GRANULARITY_KB=4 restores exact Windows behaviour without a
+ * rebuild, which is the escape hatch if some other title dislikes it.
+ * Rounding up commits memory the guest did not ask for -- up to
+ * three extra pages per commit at 16 KB -- and NtQueryVirtualMemory will report
+ * those pages as committed, which an allocator probing for free space can see.
+ * That is a real cost, paid on every commit in every container, and it is why
+ * the override exists and why this comment names the price.
+ */
+static SIZE_T commit_granularity_mask(void)
+{
+    static SIZE_T mask = ~(SIZE_T)0;
+
+    if (mask == ~(SIZE_T)0)
+    {
+        const char *e = getenv( "WINE_COMMIT_GRANULARITY_KB" );
+        unsigned int kb = e ? atoi( e ) : 16;   /* 16 KB unless told otherwise */
+
+        /* A power of two, at least one page, and not so large that a small
+         * commit turns into a megabyte. Anything else falls back to the page
+         * size, which is what Windows does. */
+        if (kb < 4 || kb > 64 || (kb & (kb - 1))) mask = page_mask;
+        else
+        {
+            mask = (SIZE_T)kb * 1024 - 1;
+            if (mask != page_mask)
+                ERR( "commit granularity is %u KB, not the page size; commits will be rounded up\n", kb );
+        }
+    }
+    return mask;
+}
+
+
 static NTSTATUS allocate_virtual_memory( void **ret, SIZE_T *size_ptr, ULONG type, ULONG protect,
                                          ULONG_PTR limit_low, ULONG_PTR limit_high,
                                          ULONG_PTR align, ULONG attributes )
@@ -5655,6 +5714,22 @@
     }
     else  /* commit the pages */
     {
+        /* Vessel: round the commit up to the container's granularity, but never
+         * past the end of the view it belongs to -- growing beyond that would
+         * turn a legal commit into STATUS_NOT_MAPPED_VIEW, which is the one way
+         * this could break a program that is doing nothing wrong. */
+        SIZE_T gran = commit_granularity_mask();
+
+        if (gran != page_mask)
+        {
+            struct file_view *whole = find_view( base, size );
+            SIZE_T grown = ROUND_SIZE( base, size, gran );
+
+            if (whole && grown > size &&
+                (char *)base + grown <= (char *)whole->base + whole->size)
+                size = grown;
+        }
+
         if (!(view = find_view( base, size ))) status = STATUS_NOT_MAPPED_VIEW;
         else if (view->protect & SEC_FILE) status = STATUS_ALREADY_COMMITTED;
         else if (view->protect & VPROT_FREE_PLACEHOLDER) status = STATUS_CONFLICTING_ADDRESSES;
